1
00:00:00,542 --> 00:00:01,157
Sir.

2
00:00:01,197 --> 00:00:03,959
Field report number two in the cryptocurrency file.

3
00:00:03,999 --> 00:00:04,875
Today's subject:

4
00:00:04,915 --> 00:00:05,648
the blockchain.

5
00:00:05,688 --> 00:00:06,984
A ledger with no guard...

6
00:00:07,024 --> 00:00:08,229
that guards itself.

7
00:00:08,302 --> 00:00:10,189
I will report how this ledger gets written,

8
00:00:10,229 --> 00:00:11,791
who guards it, and who can change its

9
00:00:11,831 --> 00:00:12,536
rules.

10
00:00:12,576 --> 00:00:14,212
And why guarding it burns a country's worth

11
00:00:14,252 --> 00:00:15,173
of electricity.

12
00:00:15,216 --> 00:00:16,212
Last report:

13
00:00:16,252 --> 00:00:18,165
bitcoin took the ledger from the bank, and

14
00:00:18,205 --> 00:00:20,251
gave a copy to thousands of computers.

15
00:00:20,291 --> 00:00:21,844
Which raises a dangerous question:

16
00:00:21,884 --> 00:00:23,329
if everyone has the ledger...

17
00:00:23,369 --> 00:00:25,446
what stops anyone writing themselves a million?

18
00:00:25,486 --> 00:00:26,776
The first trick I found is called a

19
00:00:26,816 --> 00:00:27,333
hash.

20
00:00:27,373 --> 00:00:28,848
A machine that takes any text, and gives

21
00:00:28,888 --> 00:00:29,807
back a fingerprint:

22
00:00:29,847 --> 00:00:31,082
a jumble of letters and numbers.

23
00:00:31,122 --> 00:00:32,652
Change a single letter in the text...

24
00:00:32,692 --> 00:00:34,393
and the whole fingerprint changes.

25
00:00:34,433 --> 00:00:35,785
And you can't go back from the fingerprint

26
00:00:35,825 --> 00:00:36,466
to the text.

27
00:00:36,506 --> 00:00:37,258
One way only.

28
00:00:37,298 --> 00:00:38,324
Like a meat grinder, sir.

29
00:00:38,364 --> 00:00:39,850
Steak goes in, meatballs come out.

30
00:00:39,890 --> 00:00:41,080
Meatballs never turn back into steak.

31
00:00:41,120 --> 00:00:42,904
The humans use this trick everywhere.

32
00:00:42,944 --> 00:00:44,990
A good website doesn't keep your password.

33
00:00:45,030 --> 00:00:46,737
It keeps its fingerprint.

34
00:00:46,777 --> 00:00:49,397
About every ten minutes, new transfers are gathered

35
00:00:49,437 --> 00:00:50,671
onto one page.

36
00:00:50,711 --> 00:00:51,748
They call it a block.

37
00:00:51,788 --> 00:00:53,748
One page holds a few thousand transfers.

38
00:00:53,788 --> 00:00:55,771
Then the page is stamped with its fingerprint.

39
00:00:55,811 --> 00:00:57,417
The next page starts with the fingerprint of

40
00:00:57,457 --> 00:00:58,100
the one before.

41
00:00:58,140 --> 00:01:00,026
So every page holds on to the last.

42
00:01:00,066 --> 00:01:01,075
Which makes a chain.

43
00:01:01,115 --> 00:01:01,682
Block...

44
00:01:01,722 --> 00:01:02,269
chain.

45
00:01:02,309 --> 00:01:04,189
Naming things is not their strength.

46
00:01:04,229 --> 00:01:06,705
This chain now has more than nine hundred

47
00:01:06,745 --> 00:01:09,210
thousand pages, from the first one Satoshi wrote,

48
00:01:09,250 --> 00:01:10,956
to the one written ten minutes ago.

49
00:01:10,996 --> 00:01:12,879
Imagine someone goes back and changes one number

50
00:01:12,919 --> 00:01:13,678
on an old page.

51
00:01:13,718 --> 00:01:14,880
Its fingerprint changes...

52
00:01:14,920 --> 00:01:15,807
the next page breaks...

53
00:01:15,847 --> 00:01:16,537
and the next.

54
00:01:16,577 --> 00:01:18,876
And the whole network sees a broken chain,

55
00:01:18,916 --> 00:01:19,660
and rejects it.

56
00:01:19,700 --> 00:01:21,214
It also means that what is written stays

57
00:01:21,254 --> 00:01:21,620
written.

58
00:01:21,660 --> 00:01:23,417
There is no undo button, and no clerk

59
00:01:23,457 --> 00:01:23,996
to call.

60
00:01:24,036 --> 00:01:25,638
Remember that one for the next report.

61
00:01:25,678 --> 00:01:27,576
So to forge one line, you'd have to

62
00:01:27,616 --> 00:01:29,713
rewrite every page after it.

63
00:01:31,421 --> 00:01:32,982
And here comes the cleverest part.

64
00:01:33,022 --> 00:01:34,627
I tracked one transfer from the moment it

65
00:01:34,667 --> 00:01:35,111
was sent.

66
00:01:35,151 --> 00:01:37,284
First, it is announced to the entire network.

67
00:01:37,324 --> 00:01:38,787
Then it sits in a waiting room with

68
00:01:38,827 --> 00:01:40,238
thousands of other transfers.

69
00:01:40,278 --> 00:01:41,776
And whoever pays the higher fee...

70
00:01:41,816 --> 00:01:42,546
goes in first.

71
00:01:42,586 --> 00:01:44,223
Until it's written onto a page.

72
00:01:44,263 --> 00:01:45,848
Every page written on top of it settles

73
00:01:45,888 --> 00:01:46,704
it more firmly.

74
00:01:46,744 --> 00:01:48,484
For large amounts, they wait for six pages.

75
00:01:48,524 --> 00:01:49,602
That is about an hour.

76
00:01:49,642 --> 00:01:51,257
An hour to confirm one transfer.

77
00:01:51,297 --> 00:01:52,741
And the humans consider that fast.

78
00:01:52,781 --> 00:01:53,809
Because they have used banks.

79
00:01:53,849 --> 00:01:55,866
The whole network handles about seven transfers a

80
00:01:55,906 --> 00:01:56,504
second.

81
00:01:56,544 --> 00:01:58,260
Their card networks handle thousands.

82
00:01:58,300 --> 00:01:59,945
Here, safety beats speed.

83
00:01:59,985 --> 00:02:01,427
A strange choice, for humans.

84
00:02:01,467 --> 00:02:03,315
And when the network gets crowded, the fees

85
00:02:03,355 --> 00:02:04,095
catch fire.

86
00:02:04,135 --> 00:02:05,657
On busy days they have reached tens of

87
00:02:05,697 --> 00:02:07,081
dollars for a single transfer.

88
00:02:07,121 --> 00:02:08,777
Which is why nobody buys coffee with it.

89
00:02:08,817 --> 00:02:10,864
For speed, they built a second network on

90
00:02:10,904 --> 00:02:13,114
top for small amounts, called Lightning.

91
00:02:13,154 --> 00:02:15,661
Pay instantly, settle on the big ledger later.

92
00:02:15,701 --> 00:02:17,126
Like a tab at the corner shop.

93
00:02:17,166 --> 00:02:18,735
So who writes the new page?

94
00:02:18,775 --> 00:02:20,197
And what stops that one from cheating?

95
00:02:20,237 --> 00:02:21,225
The solution I found:

96
00:02:21,265 --> 00:02:22,043
a contest.

97
00:02:22,083 --> 00:02:24,036
For your page to be accepted, its fingerprint

98
00:02:24,076 --> 00:02:25,251
has to start with a certain number of

99
00:02:25,291 --> 00:02:25,898
zeros.

100
00:02:25,938 --> 00:02:27,035
And there is no way to know in

101
00:02:27,075 --> 00:02:28,480
advance, except trying.

102
00:02:28,520 --> 00:02:29,562
Number after number.

103
00:02:29,602 --> 00:02:31,615
The whole network together tries about a thousand

104
00:02:31,655 --> 00:02:32,815
billion billion numbers...

105
00:02:32,855 --> 00:02:33,745
every single second.

106
00:02:33,785 --> 00:02:34,951
The first machine to find a number that

107
00:02:34,991 --> 00:02:35,825
works wins:

108
00:02:35,865 --> 00:02:37,403
its page joins the chain, and it gets

109
00:02:37,443 --> 00:02:37,921
a prize.

110
00:02:37,961 --> 00:02:38,629
New bitcoin.

111
00:02:38,669 --> 00:02:40,336
Everyone else checks the answer instantly.

112
00:02:40,376 --> 00:02:41,977
The answer is hard to find, and very

113
00:02:42,017 --> 00:02:42,687
easy to check.

114
00:02:42,727 --> 00:02:43,837
This is called mining.

115
00:02:43,877 --> 00:02:44,631
No pickaxe.

116
00:02:44,671 --> 00:02:45,459
No mine.

117
00:02:45,499 --> 00:02:47,973
Warehouses full of machines guessing numbers, and fans

118
00:02:48,013 --> 00:02:48,668
keeping them cool.

119
00:02:48,708 --> 00:02:50,443
The official name is proof of work.

120
00:02:50,483 --> 00:02:52,333
The fingerprint you found proves you really burned

121
00:02:52,373 --> 00:02:53,079
the electricity.

122
00:02:53,119 --> 00:02:54,738
The only certificate on this planet that cannot

123
00:02:54,778 --> 00:02:55,418
be forged.

124
00:02:55,458 --> 00:02:56,764
And if more machines join and it gets

125
00:02:56,804 --> 00:02:57,201
easier?

126
00:02:57,241 --> 00:02:58,782
The system makes it harder on its own

127
00:02:58,822 --> 00:03:00,598
every two weeks, so a page still comes

128
00:03:00,638 --> 00:03:01,401
every ten minutes.

129
00:03:01,441 --> 00:03:03,383
At the start, Satoshi mined on an ordinary

130
00:03:03,423 --> 00:03:04,071
home computer.

131
00:03:04,111 --> 00:03:06,362
Today it takes specialized machines, in warehouses the

132
00:03:06,402 --> 00:03:07,438
size of football fields.

133
00:03:07,478 --> 00:03:08,165
The math is simple:

134
00:03:08,205 --> 00:03:09,866
a machine whose electricity costs more than its

135
00:03:09,906 --> 00:03:11,049
prize gets switched off.

136
00:03:11,089 --> 00:03:12,534
So mines chase the cheapest power on the

137
00:03:12,574 --> 00:03:12,928
planet.

138
00:03:12,968 --> 00:03:13,547
Dams.

139
00:03:13,587 --> 00:03:14,939
Gas once burned off into the air.

140
00:03:14,979 --> 00:03:15,790
Even volcanoes.

141
00:03:15,830 --> 00:03:17,555
Most mining used to be in China, until

142
00:03:17,595 --> 00:03:19,056
China banned it in 2021.

143
00:03:19,096 --> 00:03:20,504
So the machines were shipped to America and

144
00:03:20,544 --> 00:03:20,892
Russia.

145
00:03:20,932 --> 00:03:22,982
These mines move in cardboard boxes.

146
00:03:23,022 --> 00:03:23,810
And China itself?

147
00:03:23,850 --> 00:03:24,385
Still mining.

148
00:03:24,425 --> 00:03:24,926
Quietly.

149
00:03:24,966 --> 00:03:26,381
There, sir, a ban is more of a

150
00:03:26,421 --> 00:03:27,051
suggestion.

151
00:03:27,091 --> 00:03:29,039
Machine owners also team up and split the

152
00:03:29,079 --> 00:03:29,645
prize.

153
00:03:29,685 --> 00:03:31,593
Right now, just two teams win about half

154
00:03:31,633 --> 00:03:32,735
of all the contests.

155
00:03:32,775 --> 00:03:33,916
A network with no boss.

156
00:03:33,956 --> 00:03:35,393
But with some very big players.

157
00:03:35,433 --> 00:03:36,745
The humans themselves are worried about that.

158
00:03:36,785 --> 00:03:38,434
And tens of thousands of computers do not

159
00:03:38,474 --> 00:03:39,113
mine at all.

160
00:03:39,153 --> 00:03:40,655
They just hold a copy of the ledger

161
00:03:40,695 --> 00:03:41,858
and check every page.

162
00:03:41,898 --> 00:03:43,363
Those are the referees.

163
00:03:43,403 --> 00:03:44,682
And nobody pays them.

164
00:03:44,722 --> 00:03:46,391
So the humans guard their money with a

165
00:03:46,431 --> 00:03:48,902
lottery that has run every ten minutes since

166
00:03:48,942 --> 00:03:49,922
2009.

167
00:03:49,962 --> 00:03:50,533
Nonstop.

168
00:03:50,573 --> 00:03:52,030
And why does this protect the ledger?

169
00:03:52,070 --> 00:03:53,919
Because a forger has to win this contest

170
00:03:53,959 --> 00:03:57,091
alone, against every machine on the planet, again

171
00:03:57,131 --> 00:03:57,661
and again.

172
00:03:57,701 --> 00:04:00,042
Which means more machines and more electricity than

173
00:04:00,082 --> 00:04:01,358
half of the entire network.

174
00:04:01,398 --> 00:04:02,922
Billions of dollars of hardware.

175
00:04:02,962 --> 00:04:04,806
Theft is possible in theory, sir.

176
00:04:04,846 --> 00:04:06,439
It just costs more than the loot.

177
00:04:06,479 --> 00:04:08,434
They call it a fifty-one percent attack.

178
00:04:08,474 --> 00:04:10,509
It has actually happened to small coins.

179
00:04:10,549 --> 00:04:11,783
To bitcoin itself:

180
00:04:11,823 --> 00:04:12,217
never.

181
00:04:12,257 --> 00:04:13,780
And if two machines win at the same

182
00:04:13,820 --> 00:04:14,318
moment?

183
00:04:14,358 --> 00:04:16,350
The network follows the longer chain, and the

184
00:04:16,390 --> 00:04:17,328
other one is forgotten.

185
00:04:17,368 --> 00:04:17,982
No meeting.

186
00:04:18,022 --> 00:04:18,566
No committee.

187
00:04:18,606 --> 00:04:19,968
The price is paid in electricity.

188
00:04:20,008 --> 00:04:22,307
By Cambridge University's estimates, the bitcoin network uses

189
00:04:22,347 --> 00:04:23,428
about as much in a year as a

190
00:04:23,468 --> 00:04:24,502
mid-sized country.

191
00:04:24,542 --> 00:04:26,328
Roughly half a percent of all the electricity

192
00:04:26,368 --> 00:04:27,016
on the planet.

193
00:04:27,056 --> 00:04:28,715
A country's worth of electricity...

194
00:04:28,755 --> 00:04:30,423
so nobody has to trust anybody.

195
00:04:30,463 --> 00:04:32,086
Trust is expensive among these creatures.

196
00:04:32,126 --> 00:04:34,120
Part of that electricity comes from sources that

197
00:04:34,160 --> 00:04:35,402
would have been wasted anyway.

198
00:04:35,442 --> 00:04:36,500
Part does not.

199
00:04:36,540 --> 00:04:38,008
The humans are still arguing about it.

200
00:04:38,048 --> 00:04:39,585
And the machines are replaced every few years,

201
00:04:39,625 --> 00:04:40,776
because the newest ones win.

202
00:04:40,816 --> 00:04:42,384
So the guarding also makes a mountain of

203
00:04:42,424 --> 00:04:43,324
junk.

204
00:04:44,632 --> 00:04:45,485
As for the prize:

205
00:04:45,525 --> 00:04:47,183
it started at fifty bitcoin a page.

206
00:04:47,223 --> 00:04:48,816
About every four years, it's cut in half.

207
00:04:48,856 --> 00:04:49,479
Fifty.

208
00:04:49,519 --> 00:04:50,198
Twenty-five.

209
00:04:50,238 --> 00:04:51,046
Twelve and a half.

210
00:04:51,086 --> 00:04:51,673
And today:

211
00:04:51,713 --> 00:04:52,511
three and a bit.

212
00:04:52,551 --> 00:04:54,484
The next halving is expected in 2028.

213
00:04:54,524 --> 00:04:55,704
The prize will be about one and a

214
00:04:55,744 --> 00:04:56,103
half.

215
00:04:56,143 --> 00:04:58,201
And anyone with expensive electricity will pack up

216
00:04:58,241 --> 00:04:58,647
and leave.

217
00:04:58,687 --> 00:05:00,073
Because of all this, the total has a

218
00:05:00,113 --> 00:05:00,533
ceiling:

219
00:05:00,573 --> 00:05:01,967
twenty-one million bitcoin.

220
00:05:02,007 --> 00:05:02,529
Never more.

221
00:05:02,569 --> 00:05:03,427
It is written in the code.

222
00:05:03,467 --> 00:05:05,287
About ninety-five percent of them have already been

223
00:05:05,327 --> 00:05:05,743
mined.

224
00:05:05,783 --> 00:05:07,381
The last one is expected around the year

225
00:05:07,421 --> 00:05:08,424
2140.

226
00:05:08,464 --> 00:05:10,259
Bitcoin number twenty million was mined in March

227
00:05:10,299 --> 00:05:11,161
2026.

228
00:05:11,201 --> 00:05:12,038
One million left...

229
00:05:12,078 --> 00:05:13,455
and it will take more than a hundred

230
00:05:13,495 --> 00:05:13,840
years.

231
00:05:13,880 --> 00:05:15,430
And when the prizes run out, the miners

232
00:05:15,470 --> 00:05:16,586
will live on fees alone.

233
00:05:16,626 --> 00:05:18,094
Will that be enough to guard the ledger?

234
00:05:18,134 --> 00:05:18,943
Nobody here knows.

235
00:05:18,983 --> 00:05:20,131
It is an open question.

236
00:05:20,171 --> 00:05:22,583
Each bitcoin splits into a hundred million pieces.

237
00:05:22,623 --> 00:05:24,036
One piece is a satoshi.

238
00:05:24,076 --> 00:05:25,163
Named after the one who left.

239
00:05:25,203 --> 00:05:26,792
Money nobody can print more of.

240
00:05:26,832 --> 00:05:28,343
On a planet that prints money whenever it's

241
00:05:28,383 --> 00:05:29,960
in trouble, that's revolutionary.

242
00:05:30,055 --> 00:05:31,517
And then I asked the question that matters

243
00:05:31,557 --> 00:05:33,158
to any intelligence service:

244
00:05:33,198 --> 00:05:34,486
who runs this network?

245
00:05:34,526 --> 00:05:35,846
Who can change the rules?

246
00:05:35,886 --> 00:05:37,073
The rules are a program.

247
00:05:37,113 --> 00:05:38,503
And every computer holding a copy of the

248
00:05:38,543 --> 00:05:40,208
ledger runs that program by choice.

249
00:05:40,248 --> 00:05:41,563
Whoever wants to change a rule writes a

250
00:05:41,603 --> 00:05:42,789
new version of the program.

251
00:05:42,829 --> 00:05:44,195
Whoever is convinced runs it.

252
00:05:44,235 --> 00:05:45,312
And if they disagree?

253
00:05:45,352 --> 00:05:46,679
The chain splits in two.

254
00:05:46,719 --> 00:05:47,642
They call it a fork.

255
00:05:47,682 --> 00:05:49,106
They named it after cutlery.

256
00:05:49,146 --> 00:05:50,566
This planet is always hungry.

257
00:05:50,606 --> 00:05:51,497
It has happened.

258
00:05:51,537 --> 00:05:53,505
In 2017, after years of fighting over the

259
00:05:53,545 --> 00:05:54,573
size of the page.

260
00:05:54,613 --> 00:05:56,289
Big companies and most of the miners wanted

261
00:05:56,329 --> 00:05:57,126
bigger pages.

262
00:05:57,166 --> 00:05:58,956
The people running the copies refused.

263
00:05:58,996 --> 00:05:59,750
The rule stayed.

264
00:05:59,790 --> 00:06:00,997
And a group of the losers took a

265
00:06:01,037 --> 00:06:02,163
copy and left:

266
00:06:02,203 --> 00:06:03,943
a new coin, Bitcoin Cash.

267
00:06:03,983 --> 00:06:05,765
Only once did everyone agree quickly.

268
00:06:05,805 --> 00:06:08,311
In 2010, a bug let someone create a

269
00:06:08,351 --> 00:06:10,125
hundred and eighty-four billion bitcoin for themselves.

270
00:06:10,165 --> 00:06:10,884
Out of thin air.

271
00:06:10,924 --> 00:06:12,663
Within about five hours, the network was running

272
00:06:12,703 --> 00:06:14,651
a fixed version, and that page was erased

273
00:06:14,691 --> 00:06:15,298
from history.

274
00:06:15,338 --> 00:06:16,380
So the ones with the machines and the

275
00:06:16,420 --> 00:06:17,733
money could not force their way.

276
00:06:17,773 --> 00:06:19,597
This network is not run by majority, sir.

277
00:06:19,637 --> 00:06:20,773
It is run by stubbornness.

278
00:06:20,813 --> 00:06:22,810
And can the twenty-one million ceiling change?

279
00:06:22,850 --> 00:06:23,888
In theory, yes.

280
00:06:23,928 --> 00:06:24,570
In practice:

281
00:06:24,610 --> 00:06:27,260
convince people who bought something because it's scarce...

282
00:06:27,300 --> 00:06:28,516
to make it less scarce.

283
00:06:28,556 --> 00:06:29,044
Good luck.

284
00:06:29,084 --> 00:06:30,776
Two words from the Human Dictionary.

285
00:06:30,816 --> 00:06:31,434
